{"id":"adadelta-update","t":"AdaDelta: an update with no global learning rate","tags":["Optimization","Training"],"d":"Medium","rt":"numpy","fn":"adadelta","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"AdaDelta keeps two running averages per parameter and rescales each step so that its units match the parameter's. Write `adadelta(A, b, x0, steps, rho=0.9, eps=1e-6)`.\n\nStart with $E[g^2] = 0$ and $E[\\Delta x^2] = 0$. Each step:\n$$E[g^2] \\leftarrow \\rho E[g^2] + (1-\\rho) g^2$$\n$$\\Delta x = -\\frac{\\sqrt{E[\\Delta x^2] + \\epsilon}}{\\sqrt{E[g^2] + \\epsilon}}\\, g$$\n$$E[\\Delta x^2] \\leftarrow \\rho E[\\Delta x^2] + (1-\\rho) \\Delta x^2, \\qquad x \\leftarrow x + \\Delta x$$\n\nThe objective is the quadratic $f(x) = \\tfrac12 x^\\top A x - b^\\top x$, so the gradient is $g = Ax - b$ (`A` is a symmetric positive-definite matrix, `b` a vector). Return the list of iterates `[x_0, x_1, ..., x_steps]` as float arrays.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef adadelta(A, b, x0, steps, rho=0.9, eps=1e-6):\n    \"\"\"Return the list of iterates, starting with x0.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Update the average of squared gradients before computing the step, and the average of squared steps after it.","At the start E[dx^2] = 0, so the first steps have size about sqrt(eps) / RMS(g): AdaDelta starts slowly and speeds up.","Everything is element-wise; each parameter gets its own effective step size."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef adadelta(A, b, x0, steps, rho=0.9, eps=1e-6):\n    A, b = np.asarray(A, dtype=float), np.asarray(b, dtype=float)\n    x = np.asarray(x0, dtype=float).copy()\n    Eg, Ed = np.zeros_like(x), np.zeros_like(x)\n    out = [x.copy()]\n    for _ in range(steps):\n        g = A @ x - b\n        Eg = rho * Eg + (1 - rho) * g * g\n        dx = -np.sqrt(Ed + eps) / np.sqrt(Eg + eps) * g\n        Ed = rho * Ed + (1 - rho) * dx * dx\n        x = x + dx\n        out.append(x.copy())\n    return out\n","ex_md":"## Units\nIn plain gradient descent the step $\\eta g$ has units of the gradient times the learning rate, not of the parameter. AdaDelta's ratio $\\sqrt{E[\\Delta x^2]}/\\sqrt{E[g^2]}$ is an approximation to the inverse curvature, like a diagonal Newton step, so the update has the parameter's units and no global learning rate is needed.\n\n## Relation to RMSProp and AdaGrad\nAdaGrad divides by the root of the **sum** of all squared gradients, so steps shrink forever. RMSProp replaces the sum with an exponential average. AdaDelta goes further and replaces the learning rate with the running RMS of past updates.\n\n## In practice\nAdaDelta's slow start (steps of size about $\\sqrt{\\epsilon}$) and its sensitivity to $\\epsilon$ mean it is rarely used for large models; Adam and AdamW dominate. It is still a useful example of how adaptive methods are built.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- What happens to the step size if gradients suddenly become very large?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[ 2.,  0.],\n       [ 0., 20.]])\narray([0., 0.])\narray([1., 1.])\n5","out":"[array([1., 1.]), array([0.99683773, 0.99683772]), array([0.99359817, 0.99359817]), array([0.99030905, 0.99030903]), array([0.98698404, 0.98698403]), array([0.98363132, 0.9836313 ])]"},{"in":"array([[2.]])\narray([6.])\narray([0.])\n40","out":"[array([0.]), array([0.00316228]), array([0.00640508]), array([0.00970201]), array([0.01304006]), array([0.01641156]), array([0.01981145]), array([0.02323614]), array([0.02668297]), array([0.0301499]), array([0.03363532]), array([0.03713791]), array([0.0406566]), array([0.04419049]), array([0.04773881]), array([0.0513009]), array([0.05487621]), array([0.05846424]), array([0.06206455]), array([0.06567677]), array([0.06930056]), array([0.0729356]), array([0.07658162]), array([0.08023838]), array([0.08390566]), array([0.08758323]), array([0.09127092]), array([0.09496856]), array([0.09867598]), ar …"}],"lessons":[],"related":["rmsprop","adagrad","adam-step","nadam-update"],"theory":[],"ntests":5,"tj":"[{\"a\